#!/usr/bin/env ork.python
################################################################################
# Terrain chunk renderer THREE-WAY A/B: SSBO-pull VS (+cull compute) vs taskless
# MESH SHADER, dispatched two ways.
#
# All three paths are generated by ONE source of truth — TerrainChunkVertexSource
# (gpu_chunk.py) — over ONE SSBO layout and ONE procedural heightfield:
#
#   vtx  (baseline) : cs_terrain_reset/cull/sort/finalize fill v_list + a
#                     VkDrawIndirectCommand; DrawIndirectEML pulls chunk^2*6
#                     non-indexed vertices, so every interior corner is decoded
#                     (and its heights[] tapped) ~6x.
#   mesh (direct)   : NO compute and NO indirect args. A FIXED workgroup grid
#                     (mesh_groups()) covers chunks x meshlets; each workgroup
#                     frustum-tests its own n x n-quad meshlet AABB and either
#                     emits nothing (SetMeshOutputsEXT(0,0)) or emits its (n+1)^2
#                     unique corners ONCE plus 2n^2 local-index triangles (n =
#                     gpu_chunk.DEFAULT_MESHLET_DIM, 8 -> 81 verts / 128 tris). A
#                     CULLED meshlet still costs a dispatched workgroup — which
#                     is why the all-culled view LOSES to the baseline on
#                     MoltenVK (mps^2 * nchunk workgroups that do nothing).
#   mesh (indirect) : ONE compaction dispatch (cs_terrain_meshcull) writes the
#                     compacted visible-chunk list AND the mesh draw's
#                     VkDrawMeshTasksIndirectCommandEXT{x=mps^2,y=visible,z=1};
#                     drawMeshTasksIndirect reads its grid from there, so the
#                     all-culled view dispatches literally nothing. Per-meshlet
#                     self-cull is retained inside the surviving chunks.
#
# Same triangulation, same winding, same per-vertex math => all three paths must
# rasterize the SAME image. This gate has two INDEPENDENT halves:
#
#   CORRECTNESS (trusted, unchanged methodology): pixel parity at 256x256 across
#     wide/near/away, measured against the BASELINE for every mesh path (a small
#     tolerance covers depth ties the mesh paths break differently — no sort). When
#     the device has the indirect draw entry it also asserts the compaction agrees
#     chunk-for-chunk with the baseline cull and that the all-culled view dispatches
#     literally NOTHING (y=0). Emits TESTVERDICT.
#
#   PERFORMANCE (reworked, owner spec 2026-07-24): GPU-honest per-frame wall-clock
#     at 2560x1280 offscreen. See the MESHPERF methodology block below. The prior
#     sub-ms perf_counter-sandwich-with-baseline-subtraction methodology (which
#     produced negative draw-times and an inverted "speedup") is REMOVED, not
#     deprecated.
#
# Fully synthetic: the heightfield is procedural and the shader is inline text —
# NO scene assets, NO asset cache, NO window. Runtime-gated on the real caps
# queries (ctx.supports_mesh_shader / .supports_mesh_shader_indirect): a device
# without VK_EXT_mesh_shader SKIPs clean, and one without the indirect draw entry
# runs the two-way, so the canary stays green fleet-wide.
################################################################################
#
# MESHPERF METHODOLOGY (binding, owner-specified 2026-07-24)
# ---------------------------------------------------------
# TIMING (GPU-honest, no sub-ms sandwiches):
#   In offscreen mode VkOffscreen::submit() does queueSubmit + fence->wait() per
#   frame (vulkan_offscreen.cpp). So host wall-clock wrapped around ONE full
#   frame() call — beginFrame .. draw .. endFrame — includes the whole GPU
#   submission AND the completion sync. We render N (>=300) full frames per leg,
#   collect the per-frame wall samples, and report mean / p50 / p95. No baseline
#   subtraction, no per-pass batching, no negative arithmetic.
#
# RASTER LOAD: perf legs run at 2560x1280 (--res) to lift real rasterization out
#   of the sub-ms noise floor. Correctness legs stay at 256x256 (--parity-res)
#   for speed; parity does not need the load.
#
# VSYNC-FREE / LOAD GUARD: before any timing a trivial calibration (empty clear
#   frames at perf res) must exceed --calib-min-fps (default 120). Offscreen is
#   unpaced by design; a rate below the floor means the box is paced or overloaded
#   — the gate then REFUSES to measure (prints reason=paced_or_overloaded and the
#   measured rate, emits ZERO MESHPERF lines). Under heavy box load this refusal
#   is the CORRECT outcome, not a bug; it does not redden the correctness verdict.
#
# SYMMETRIC ACCOUNTING: each path is timed doing exactly the per-frame work it
#   actually requires.
#     vtx      : full reset->cull->sort->finalize dispatch phase (submit+wait) THEN
#                the indirect draw frame — both round-trips inside the timed window.
#     mesh     : the fixed-grid mesh-task draw frame, NO compute.
#     mesh_ind : ONE compaction dispatch recorded INLINE onto the frame CB (dispatch +
#                producer->consumer barrier, NO separate submit/fence) THEN the indirect
#                mesh-task draw frame — one submit total. The compaction is mode 2's OWN
#                per-frame work, charged to the mesh_ind leg (never to vtx or direct mesh).
#   There are NO HUD/stats compute passes in this synthetic test (the CullStats HUD
#   passes live in the real terrain drawable, not here), so "HUD off" holds by
#   construction.
#
# RATIO is DEFINED as mesh_time / vtx_time  (values < 1.0 => mesh FASTER). No more
#   inverted "speedup". When the indirect leg runs, ratio_ind = mesh_ind / vtx (same
#   convention) is appended.
#
# HARD FAILS: any per-frame sample <= 0, or a non-positive mean/p95, aborts the
#   gate with reason=timing_artifact.
#
# OUTPUT: one machine-parseable line per vantage:
#   MESHPERF vantage=<v> res=<WxH> frames=<N> vtx_ms_mean=.. vtx_ms_p50=.. \
#            vtx_ms_p95=.. mesh_ms_mean=.. mesh_ms_p50=.. mesh_ms_p95=.. ratio=.. \
#            [mesh_ind_ms_mean=.. mesh_ind_ms_p50=.. mesh_ind_ms_p95=.. ratio_ind=..]
################################################################################
#
# MSAA PER-SAMPLE SURCHARGE LEG (added 2026-07-26)
# -----------------------------------------------
# WHAT IT MEASURES: the same mesh-vs-baseline comparison, run into a 1x target and
#   into an MSAA target (default 4x, --msaa-samples) at the SAME resolution and the
#   SAME vantage. Everything else — SSBO, shader text, camera, frame count — is held
#   identical, so the only variable is the render target's sample count.
#
# THE METRIC IS A RATIO OF RATIOS:
#   measured at 3840x2160 (--surcharge-res), NOT at the MESHPERF res: a per-SAMPLE defect only
#   separates from the per-frame submit floor once samples are the dominant work (SURCHARGE_W/H).
#     ratio_1x  = mesh_ms(1x)  / vtx_ms(1x)
#     ratio_Nx  = mesh_ms(Nx)  / vtx_ms(Nx)
#     surcharge = ratio_Nx / ratio_1x
#   surcharge > 1.0 means the mesh pipeline's MSAA cost multiplier exceeds the vertex
#   pipeline's over identical geometry. It is a RELATIVE measure and its denominator is
#   a real pipeline with its own MSAA behaviour — do NOT read it as "the mesh pipeline is
#   broken by X". At the SHIPPED meshlet 8 it measures 1.29 on NVIDIA/linux and 1.15 on
#   MoltenVK/M3 Ultra, and NEITHER absolute 1x->4x cost is alarming: mesh pays +0.85ms on
#   NVIDIA / +0.39ms on mac, its own vertex baseline +0.71ms / +0.46ms. The ratio exceeds 1
#   on both platforms mostly because the mesh path's 1x frame is the CHEAPER one, so the same
#   added sample work is a bigger multiple of it — which is why this leg is a per-platform
#   REGRESSION detector and not a defect proof.
#
# PER-PLATFORM REGRESSION FENCES (ONE rule, two recorded baselines):
#   Each platform records the MEAN surcharge it measures at the SHIPPED meshlet, and the leg
#   fences a symmetric band around it (SURCHARGE_BASELINE / SURCHARGE_BAND):
#     surcharge > baseline * 1.15  -> FAIL     — a real regression (engine, codegen or driver).
#     surcharge < baseline * 0.85  -> IMPROVED — the world moved the other way: re-measure and
#        re-record. It does NOT fail; an improvement must never redden the fleet.
#   The band swallows run-to-run noise on both platforms with room to spare — 3 sigma over six
#   consecutive full-gate runs is 12.5% of the mean on NVIDIA and 3.7% on MoltenVK.
#   NEITHER ARM IS A KNOWN-BAD CEILING ANY MORE. At meshlet 11 darwin measured 1.59-1.72 against
#   NVIDIA's 1.10-1.37, and the darwin arm was a known-bad ceiling that could only ask "did it get
#   WORSE?". The meshlet 11 -> 8 reshape erased that gap and inverted it — darwin now measures
#   LOWER than NVIDIA (1.148 vs 1.293) — so the magnitude the ceiling encoded is gone and both arms
#   are ordinary regression detectors. The runs behind that claim are in the RECORDED BASELINES
#   block below; update the baselines WITH the measurement that justifies the change.
#
# PROOF OF LOAD (two guards, because neither alone covers the configuration):
#   coverage : one captured frame per path into an RGBA8 MSAA target of the same sample
#     count — proves the multisampled render pass actually rasterizes this geometry.
#     RGBA8 because captureAsFormat cannot read a half-float attachment back.
#   clear-floor : each timed leg must cost >1.2x an empty (clear-only) frame on the SAME
#     target it is timed against — that is the guard that covers the RGBA16F targets.
#
# OUTPUT:
#   MESHMSAA vantage=<v> res=<WxH> fmt=<F> msaa=<N> frames=<N> vtx_ms_mean=.. mesh_ms_mean=.. ratio=..
#   MESHMSAA_SURCHARGE vantage=<v> res=<WxH> fmt=<F> msaa=<N> ratio_1x=.. ratio_Nx=.. \
#            surcharge=.. baseline=.. fence=.. platform=<darwin|other> result=<PASS|FAIL|IMPROVED>
#
# REPRODUCER MODES (upstream artifacts; NOT gates — they emit no TESTVERDICT):
#   --reproduce            per-sample mesh cost: msaa x resolution slope table for both
#                          pipelines. Run it on BOTH a MoltenVK box and a discrete-GPU box —
#                          the cross-platform pair is the evidence, one platform alone is not.
#   --reproduce-indirect   the MoltenVK indirect defect: drawMeshTasksIndirect against an
#                          SSBO-bound pipeline costs ~93ms/call in Release, independent
#                          of resolution, while the SAME pipeline drawn DIRECTLY over
#                          the same compacted list costs ordinary milliseconds. Sweeps
#                          draw-count (per-call linearity) and resolution.
################################################################################
#
# PAYLOAD CLIFF FENCE (added 2026-07-26) — ONE leg, two platform arms
# --------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT IT FENCES: the tile-contention regression class, permanently. Metal tile memory is
#   shared between the multisampled attachment set and the mesh stage's per-workgroup output
#   payload, and the mesh path's 4x cost falls off a CLIFF as the payload shrinks. The
#   shipped meshlet (gpu_chunk.DEFAULT_MESHLET_DIM, bound to the C++ kTerrainDefaultMeshletDim)
#   sits just under that cliff. Anything that re-crosses it — a payload bump, a codegen change
#   that re-fattens the workgroup output, a driver regression — turns this leg red.
#
# THE MEASUREMENT (identical on both platforms, so the arms differ only in the constant):
#   the SWEEP-PAYLOAD configuration — 3840x2160, 2x RGBA16F + Z32F (what ForwardPbrNodeImpl
#   multisamples), 4x, vantage `wide`, at the DEFAULT meshlet. Reported as mesh_ms, compared
#   against the RECORDED baseline for this platform:
#
#     ratio = mesh_ms / baseline_ms ;  FAIL when ratio > fence
#
# RECORDED BASELINES (measured jul26 on the tip that flipped the default 11 -> 8; 60 frames
# per leg, same config as above, mesh_ms mean):
#
#             platform            meshlet 11   meshlet 9   meshlet 8 (SHIPPED)   cliff 11->8
#   M3 Ultra / MoltenVK / darwin      4.529       4.596      1.613 / 1.675          2.81x
#   RTX 3090 / linux                  2.630       2.676      2.651 / 2.652          1.01x (FLAT)
#   (two meshlet-8 values = two independent runs; the pair is the run-to-run spread)
#
#   darwin arm  — THE CLIFF FENCE. baseline = the shipped payload's own cost (1.63ms class),
#     fence 1.60. The cliff value is 2.78x that baseline, so a re-crossing is caught with
#     enormous margin while run-to-run drift (measured 1.61..1.68 = 1.03x) is nowhere near it.
#     The fence is deliberately loose in RATIO terms because the absolute number sits at the
#     frame floor here — a clear-only frame on the same target costs 1.88ms, i.e. 1.15x the
#     baseline, so anything short of a real cliff cannot redden this.
#   non-darwin arm — THE NO-REGRESSION ASSERTION for the default flip. baseline = the cost of
#     the payload the flip REPLACED (meshlet 11, 2.63ms), fence 1.10. NVIDIA is payload-FLAT
#     over this range (8 vs 11 = 1.008), so the smaller shipped payload must cost the
#     discrete-GPU platform nothing; 1.10 is generous against noise and far tighter than any
#     real payload effect would be.
#
# Both constants are MEASURED, not guessed — update them WITH the measurement that justifies
# the change, and keep the table above in step.
#
# OUTPUT:
#   MESHCLIFF res=<WxH> fmt=<F> nattach=<N> msaa=<N> meshlet=<n> frames=<N> clear_ms=.. \
#             vtx_ms=.. mesh_ms=.. baseline_ms=.. ratio=.. fence=.. platform=<darwin|other> \
#             result=<PASS|FAIL>
################################################################################

